WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
Let’s talk about what’s been happening in Chile.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.640
This is a country some people have described as

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.400
an economic success story.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.000
But we’re also seeing this

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.800
protesters angry at not being able to put food on the table

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.080
get proper healthcare

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.320
and just generally with how the country is run.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.560
So what are they seeing that others aren’t?

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.880
Is there a flip side to Chile’s success story?

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.320
Whether Chile can be described as successful

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.600
depends on who you ask and ultimately

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:35.400
where their politics lie.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.440
But let’s start with a look at Chile’s economy.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.080
And really any story about the country’s modern economy

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.960
should start with these guys

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.400
the Chicago Boys.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.320
They’re a famous, some would say infamous

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.760
group of Chilean neo-liberal economists.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:51.240
In the 1970s they studied in Chicago 

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.280
under Milton Friedman, a champion of

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.120
free-market capitalism and an eventual Nobel laureate.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.200
It was around that time back in Chile

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.400
that General Augusto Pinochet staged

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.000
a military coup to get rid of President Salvador Allende

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.840
and his socialist government.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.840
Chile’s economy was a mess.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:11.600
Yearly inflation was running around 300%

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.040
 and a lot of people struggled to pay for basic necessities.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.840
So Pinochet needed a plan to fix the economy

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.840
and the Chicago Boys had one.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:20.680
It went like this.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.280
The best social public policy

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:23.280
is economic growth.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.400
If the economy grows

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.360
you will have less poverty

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:27.640
and so on along the time.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.920
In this part

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.400
Chicago Boys

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.400
their recommendation was right.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.200
Chile’s economy did grow.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.960
Thanks to freeing up the market Chile made trade deals

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.760
 with countries around the world, exporting things like

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.120
copper and its internationally renowned wines.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.040
In 2010 Chile even became the first Latin American country

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.480
to join the OECD, a club for developed economies.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.120
“Applause, backslapping and especially congratulations

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.640
were in order at Chile’s presidential palace.”

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.880
Chile’s economy has had its bumps 

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.240
but compared to its Latin American neighbours

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.840
the country has become a sort of Promised Land.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.640
Between 2017 and 2018 Chile’s economy 

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.480
grew by about $20 billion.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:11.960
Compare that to Argentina

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.160
where the economy shrank by $123 billion in that time.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.320
It’s no wonder tens of thousands of migrants

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.440
from all over South America have moved to Chile

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.360
for jobs in the last few years.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.960
This is one of the leading countries in GDP per capita.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.320
We have reduced our poverty from

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:35.280
nearly 40% in 1990 down to 8.6% last year. 

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.400
So with that rosy picture in mind 

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:42.560
it’s tough to imagine why a  four cent increase

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.560
in metro ticket prices led to 

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.080
the biggest protests in Chile in years.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.680
Young people kicked it off by refusing to pay for the metro.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:54.840
Soon the protests grew.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.000
And spread.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.440
“The unrest on the streets of the Chilean capital

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:02.680
and elsewhere in the country has been unrelenting.”

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:05.320
But it also got ugly.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.160
People started burning stuff, there was looting

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.000
and the government deployed the military.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.080
Protesters and police confronted each other.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.920
Thousands were injured on both sides

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.280
and people have died.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.600
Now this movement has been about a lot of things

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:26.880
but one of the big ones is inequality.

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.560
33% of Chilean GDP goes to the 1 percenters.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.720
That's higher than almost everywhere.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:36.920
I don't think there's another country that

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.960
has that level of inequality.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:40.800
What many protesters say is that

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.760
the middle class in Chile isn’t middle class at all.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.680
That’s because in the government’s eyes

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.760
a person can qualify as middle class

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.080
even if they earn as little as $180 a month.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:54.120
For a family of four it’s about $630.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:57.600
I would be classified among the richest 20% in the country.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:03.680
And my monthly income is about 950 euros a month

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:05.720
and I’m a professional with a master's degree.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:07.680
I don’t think that’s a rich person.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.560
Chile’s National Statistics Institute calculated that

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.920
80% of people have no money left at the end of the month.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.560
Once they get through paying for food, transport

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.320
housing and basic services it’s all gone.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.640
Unless they pay for stuff in instalments

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.520
which in Chile you can do just about anywhere.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.840
A lot of people go to the supermarket

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.000
with a credit card.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.720
They don't even have enough to pay for

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:34.120
that supermarket bill in cash.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.040
So in a way, we have seen a decrease in poverty

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:41.040
but we have more people in debt.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.600
The protesters have another criticism

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.080
and that’s against high levels of privatisation.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:51.520
In Chile utilities like water and electricity

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.560
are in the hands of private companies.

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.560
Whereas sectors like education and health

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.520
have a mix of private and public options.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:01.640
Critics say that system is driving prices up

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.600
and that the quality of public offerings are worsening.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.080
If a retiree hasn’t paid into a private pension, for instance

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.320
they get a government allowance

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.360
of just $180 a month.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.880
We have people who are receiving pensions

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:21.360
after working their whole lives which are making them poor.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.400
They don’t cover their basic needs

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.680
with the pension that they are getting.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:28.480
Access to good healthcare.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.200
That is really expensive.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.040
Access to private schools.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.520
That's really expensive.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:36.880
Access to some universities.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:37.680
That's really expensive.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.800
Now in response the Chilean government has raised

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.480
the minimum wage, promised more money

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.800
for health services and the very elderly.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.720
But some protesters say it won’t be enough.

00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:50.280
And now there’s division over just how much

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.840
Chile’s free-market model should change.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.400
There are two perspectives for the crisis in Chile.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:05:58.920
One is Chile’s a poor country

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.520
unequal society and so we need to change everything.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.960
The other is much more social democrat.

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:06.880
We need to improve the pension funds

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.480
the health and so on

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:09.680
but we need to keep

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.920
the competitiveness of the economy.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.920
And that’s the government’s message

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:17.160
Yes, we recognise the need for social fairness but

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.880
We cannot change things overnight

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:22.520
and also we need to keep a country

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.920
that keeps an economy working

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:29.720
because if the discussion is about redistributing wealth

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:31.720
we first need to create that wealth.

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.440
Moving ahead won’t be easy.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:36.800
There’s been damage.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.680
Chile’s National Human Rights Institute accuses the police

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.640
and military of serious abuses that include

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:45.360
using excessive force during the demonstrations.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.440
Even Chile’s famously stable economy

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.600
had its biggest drop in a decade 

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:52.440
and probably won’t recover for a while.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.800
But there is a plan to reconcile the country.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.000
It involves rewriting the constitution that

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.000
was originally drafted during Pinochet’s dictatorship.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.920
Polls in Chile say it’s something 80% of Chileans

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.520
seem to support and so does the president.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.600
In April a referendum will ask voters whether they want

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.400
a new constitution and whether it should be written

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.720
by an all-citizen body or include lawmakers too.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.160
If it goes ahead elections for that body

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:39.480
are expected to happen in October.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.320
It’s going to be a long and politically difficult process

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:46.200
but the idea is that Chile can write a new story if you will.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:49.000
A story more people can call a success.

